  "text": "Rivers shape valleys through the steady work of erosion, transport, and deposition. In their upper reaches, fast, steep streams cut downward, using sand, pebbles, and boulders as tools to scour the bed. This vertical incision carves narrow, V-shaped valleys with steep sides, often punctuated by rapids and waterfalls. As the river descends and the gradient eases, it begins to swing from side to side, undercutting banks on the outside of bends and depositing sediment on the inside. This lateral erosion gradually widens the valley floor, creating broad meanders and flat floodplains. During floods, rivers spread silt across these plains, building fertile layers over time. Tributaries add their own channels, branching the landscape into intricate drainage networks. Over thousands to millions of years, changes in climate, sea level, and tectonic uplift can cause rivers to cut deeper again, leaving old floodplains stranded as terraces. In this way, valleys record the long, patient history of the water that formed them.",
